eing tested for thrombophilia  inappropriately and so they continued to  attempt to educate because of his work  and the work of his group dr. Zarate was  awarded the choosing wisely champion  status because he discovered  approximately 85% of thrombophilia tests  at UT Southwestern Stu Teaching  Hospitals were ordered incorrectly or  incompletely thrombophilia tests are  frequently ordered for patients with  acute thrombotic events often while  antah on anticoagulation therapy however  these additional variables cause these  standard tests to return false positive  results  these abnormal results by false positive  they mean low or deficient results by  the way these are normal results are not  always checked for reproducibility or  accuracy causing some patients to be  inappropriately placed on long-term  anticoagulation therapy to promote  appropriate use of testing dr. Cyril  Dee's team developed local guidelines  and implemented them in the electronic  health record via a series of cascading  questions that providers must answer  before ordering after implementation of  the intervention and the associated  education campaign UT Southwestern has  now reduced testing for inpatients by  more than 90 percent and this has also  resulted in to choosing wisely  recommendations that are roughly  parallel one by the American Society for  hematology their logo right here in  which they say don't test for  thrombophilia in adult patients with  venous thromboembolism occurring in the  setting of major transient risk factors  such as surgery trauma or prolonged  immobility and you can read the rest of  that recommendation yourself and because  another even more specific  recommendation came from a SCP in which  they say do not test for protein s  protein C or antithrombin during an  active clotting event to diagnose a  hereditary deficiency these tests are  not analytically accurate during an  active clotting event  I'm currently dealing with a question on  fritzman factor 