ociation of blood banks  is blood usage recommendation and they  say don't transfuse more units of blood  than absolutely necessary back in the  80s and 90s the rule was if you're going  to transfuse you better give them to not  just one because one isn't likely enough  that has been completely reversed and  now the recommendation is to restrict  blood to when the hemoglobin level is  low much slide shows the recommendation  from choosing wisely at 7 to 8 grams of  hemoglobin but many positions even use 6  grams of hemoglobin and to use only a  single unit of red blood cells for that  treatment that's a typical  recommendation now I won't read the next  three slides to you I'll give you the  opportunity to look at these themselves  but this represents a recommendation by  a FCP that was sent to Consumer Reports  and was made of and is made available to  the lay public and it's a recommendation  about vitamin  detesting and it illustrates that we do  way too much vitamin D testing wasting a  lot of money and it is pitched not to  the physician but to the lay individual  it's in intelligent terms but in lay  terms that most people can read and they  say I'll just read the bottom of this  slide says many people have low levels  of vitamin D but few have seriously low  levels most of us don't need a vitamin D  test we just need to make simple changes  so we get enough vitamin D you need to  get a little more Sun eat foods rich in  vitamin D or take a supplement and the  recommendation goes on it says extra  tests lead to extra treatment and costs  getting tests that you don't need often  leads to treatments you don't need or  treatments that can even be harmful for  example if you take too much vitamin D  it can damage your kidneys and other  organs this is continued on this third  slide and I won't really read that  that's available for you to take a look  at what I want to get to is this the  choosing wisely people are always  looking for more societies to  participate if your society would like  to participate they need to submit 5  recommendations for consideration  they must begin with the terms don't or  avoid they have to be evidence-based  with current references they must  address frequent diagnostic or  therapeutic choices they're developed  within the contributing society using  their own processes and they may send  recommendations that overlap with other  existing recommendations or conversely  they should try to avoid complete  redundancy choosing wisely  recommendations are developed with  transparency the method is summarized  with the recommendation they're  accompanied by a description of the  contributing Society  and the foundation sent seats  recommendation to two independent  reviewers for comment and approval the  foundation then sends recommendations to  all the partners to ensure that there's  no conflict with existing  recommendations and when the  recommendations are finally published  the copyright actually belongs to the  contributing society so our last  question is is choosing wisely working  and just in October on October 25 2017  just a week ago there was a working  study that looked at the outcomes of the  choosing wisely activity only one in  four US doctors knows about choosing  wisely  it was 21% in 2014 now it's 25% there  was a slight non significant increase  since 2014 in the percentage who  reported difficulty having conversations  about avoiding low value services from  42 percent to 46 percent in 2017 and I'd  like to read to you the statement that  was issued by the meeting on the 25th  they said the choosing wisely campaign  represents a practical expression of the  core mission of the ABIM foundation to  improve healthcare by strengthening  medical professionalism  according to dr. Richard Barron  president and chief executive officer of  the ABIM and the foundation he said this  in his introductory remarks he continued  how do you get from the aspiration to do  the right thing to act